a and 潤 = Moist, rūkṣa and 燥 = Dry.", "sources": [{"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app moisture (source dry, app moist).", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": null, "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "Mullein is of temperature dry: the leaves have also a digesting and cleansing quality, as Galen affirmeth.", "gloss": "Mullein is of temperature dry.", "locator": "Book II, Chap. 270 'Of Mullein', The Temperature", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "Moisture only; Gerard gives mullein no heat or cold. The clause credited to Galen is the one about digesting and cleansing, not the dryness.", "short": "Gerard (1633)", "source": "John Gerard, The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes, enlarged by Thomas Johnson (1633 text; read in the Ex-Classics transcription and checked against the 1636 printing scanned at archive.org)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt", "year": "1633"}, {"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app moisture (source dry, app moist).", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": null, "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "Verbascum, Thapsus Barbatus. Mullin, or Higtaper. It is something dry, and of a digesting, cleansing quality, stops fluxes and the hemorrhoids, it cures hoarseness, the cough, and such as are broken winded.", "gloss": "Mullein is something dry.", "locator": "Catalogue of simples, Herbs, 'Verbascum' (Gutenberg #49513, offset ~899050)", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "Moisture only. Mullein appears in none of his degree tables for heat or cold.", "short": "Culpeper (1653)", "source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (text of 1653; London: Thomas Kelly, 1850 edition as digitised by Project Gutenberg #49513)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt", "year": "1653"}, {"agreement": "Counted: agrees with the app temperature (neutral); differs from the app moisture (source dry, app moist).", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Neutral", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "IX. The Qualities. Mullein is Temperate in refpect to heat or cold, and dry in the firft Degree: Aperitive, Aftringent, PeStoral, Hyfterick, and Nephritick.", "gloss": "Mullein is temperate as to heat or cold and dry in the first degree.", "locator": "Chapter 'Of MULLEIN', The Qualities (OCR at offset 4041347; the chapter heading above it is printed as 'CHAP. DVIL' in the scan, so the number is not reliable)", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "", "short": "Salmon (1710)", "source": "William Salmon, Botanologia: The English Herbal (London, 1710)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://archive.org/download/gri_33125012917387/gri_33125012917387_djvu.txt", "year": "1710"}], "sources_agree": false, "stored_before": {"moisture": "Moist", "temperature": "Neutral"}, "stored_value_status": {"moisture": {"status": "clear", "why": "a definite Moist"}, "temperature": {"status": "clear", "why": "a definite Neutral"}}}$j$::jsonb);

-- H042 Nettle (3 sources, SOURCES DISAGREE)
insert into _energetics_batch4 values ('H042', $j${"agreement_state": "disagree", "app_value": {"moisture": "Dry", "temperature": "Cool"}, "app_value_changed": {"moisture": false, "temperature": false}, "app_value_lines": {"moisture": "stays Dry (clear modern value; old sources agree)", "temperature": "stays Cool (clear modern value; old sources disagree, shown on page)"}, "batch": 4, "checked_no_reading": ["Dymock, Pharmacographia Indica Vol. III, Urtica: uses only, no thermal quality", "Rafinesque, Medical Flora: his nettle entries are American species", "Bencao Gangmu 蕁麻, Bencao Congxin, Shennong Bencao Jing, Bhavaprakasha, Rajanighantu: no Urtica dioica entry found"], "counted_readings": {"moisture": [{"reading": "Dry", "source": "Gerard (1633)"}], "temperature": [{"reading": "Warm", "source": "Gerard (1633)"}, {"reading": "Warm", "source": "Culpeper (1653)"}]}, "disagreement_text": "On temperature, Gerard (1633) and Culpeper (1653) read it as warm. The app keeps cool, the modern reading of what the herb does in the body; the old readings are shown here but do not change it.", "no_counted_source_line": "", "no_pre1900_source_found": false, "notes": ["The stored parts are leaf, root and seed; Gerard and Culpeper describe the herb."], "part_used": "Leaf, Root, Seed", "researched": "2026-09-15", "rule": "Temperature: stored value clear (a definite Cool), so it stays Cool. Moisture: stored value clear (a definite Dry), so it stays Dry.", "rule_general": "FINAL founder rules 2026-09-15: clear stored modern value stays; unclear or missing value set by majority of first-hand pre-1900 sources; tie broken by the oldest first-hand written source, Neutral if the oldest sources are themselves tied; copied readings count once; group statements, second-hand reports, other parts and other species shown but not counted; snigdha and 潤 = Moist, rūkṣa and 燥 = Dry.", "sources": [{"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app temperature (source warm, app cool); agrees with the app moisture (dry).", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "Nettle is of temperature dry, a little hot, scarce in the first degree: it is of thin and subtle parts; for it doth not therefore burn and sting by reason it is extreme hot, but because the down of it is stiff and hard, piercing like fine little prickles or stings", "gloss": "Nettle is dry and a little hot, scarcely in the first degree, and its sting comes from the stiff down rather than from heat.", "locator": "Book II, Chap. 238 'Of Stinging Nettle', The Temperature", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "", "short": "Gerard (1633)", "source": "John Gerard, The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes, enlarged by Thomas Johnson (1633 text; read in the Ex-Classics transcription and checked against the 1636 printing scanned at archive.org)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt", "year": "1633"}, {"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app temperature (source warm, app cool).", "assigns_moisture": null, "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "Urtica. Nettles: an herb so well known, that you may find them by the feeling in the darkest night: they are something hot, not very hot; the juice stops bleeding; they provoke lust, help difficulty of breathing; and in his degree tables, 'In the third degree. … Nettles' among the hot herbs", "gloss": "Nettles are something hot, not very hot; his degree table puts them among the herbs hot in the third degree.", "locator": "Catalogue of simples, Herbs, 'Urtica' (Gutenberg #49513, offset ~900668); Culpeper's degree tables, 'Herbs' (Gutenberg #49513, offset ~950000)", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "Temperature only; nettles are in none of his dry or moist lists. The two places disagree about how hot, which is shown here rather than resolved: the catalogue says 'not very hot' and the table says the third degree.", "short": "Culpeper (1653)", "source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (text of 1653; London: Thomas Kelly, 1850 edition as digitised by Project Gutenberg #49513)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt", "year": "1653"}, {"agreement": "Shown for context. The two columns of this page are interleaved in the scan and the sentence is broken beyond repair: the words that would carry hot or cold, and the degree they belong to, are missing. Reported as damaged; nothing is reconstructed.", "assigns_moisture": null, "assigns_temperature": null, "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "IX. The Qualities. Nettle … the firft Degree, and is of t… now it does not burn and … tream heat; but becaufe it … are hard and ftiff, piercir… ", "gloss": "Salmon's Qualities sentence for the nettle, as far as the scan can be read: a first degree, and the sting explained by the hard stiff prickles rather than by extreme heat.", "locator": "Chapter 'Of NETTLE', The Qualities (OCR at offset 4191792)", "not_counted_why": "The two columns of this page are interleaved in the scan and the sentence is broken beyond repair: the words that would carry hot or cold, and the degree they belong to, are missing. Reported as damaged; nothing is reconstructed.", "note": "The two columns of this page are interleaved in the scan and the sentence is broken beyond repair: the words that would carry hot or cold, and the degree they belong to, are missing. Reported as damaged; nothing is reconstructed.", "short": "Salmon (1710)", "source": "William Salmon, Botanologia: The English Herbal (London, 1710)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://archive.org/download/gri_33125012917387/gri_33125012917387_djvu.txt", "year": "1710"}], "sources_agree": false, "stored_before": {"moisture": "Dry", "temperature": "Cool"}, "stored_value_status": {"moisture": {"status": "clear", "why": "a definite Dry"}, "temperature": {"status": "clear", "why": "a definite Cool"}}}$j$::jsonb);

